<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhimtal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhimtal is a lake and town in the Nainital district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> hills about 22 kilometres from Nainital. The lake is one of the largest in the region, and on its shore stands the Bhimeshwar temple, attributed to the 17th-century Chand ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baz-bahadur-chand">Baz Bahadur Chand</a>. It is now a popular hill resort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhimtal.jpg" alt="Bhimtal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krishan09 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhimtal_Lake.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bhimtal lies at 1,370 metres in the Outer Lesser Himalayas, 22 kilometres east of Nainital, 21 kilometres north of the railway terminus at Kathgodam and 30 kilometres north of Haldwani. It is built around Bhimtal Lake, a natural freshwater lake of 47.8 hectares, the largest by surface area in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> division. The basin is tectonic in origin: movements along transverse faults in the Quaternary period and ancestral rockslides blocked the streams and impounded the water.</p><p>The name joins Bhima, the second Pandava prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, to tal, meaning lake. Regional Puranic tradition says that during the twelve years of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandavas</a> of forest exile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draupadi">Draupadi</a> grew thirsty, and Bhima struck his iron mace into the rock and opened a spring that filled the hollow. The five brothers are said to have consecrated the Bhimeshwar Mahadev shrine on the southern shore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the seventh to the eleventh century the region was ruled by the Katyuri kings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joshimath">Joshimath</a> and Baijnath, and Bhimtal served as a military and trade outpost on the tracks between the lowlands and the Himalayan valleys. It later passed to the Chand kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champawat">Champawat</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a>, for whom it was a staging post on the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> and Almora. In 1667 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baz-bahadur-chand">Baz Bahadur Chand</a>, who reigned from 1638 to 1678, commissioned the reconstruction of the Bhimeshwar Mahadev Temple and gave tax-free land grants to support its rituals and priesthood.</p><p>In 1790 forces of the Gorkha Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> crossed the Kali River, overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chand-dynasty">Chand dynasty</a> and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> for twenty-five years. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> of 1814 to 1816 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sugauli">Treaty of Sugauli</a> of 1815, Kumaon passed to the British East India Company. Bhimtal became a rest camp and dak bungalow station on the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a>, Ranikhet and Nainital, and in 1883 the Public Works Department's Irrigation Branch completed the 485-metre Victoria Dam across the lake's outlet to supply the canals of the Bhabar tract.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bhimtal is a municipal town and the headquarters of a tehsil in the Nainital district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, with a population of 7,722 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. In October 2023 the state government raised it from a Nagar Panchayat to a Nagar Palika Parishad. The town has the Folk Culture Museum, founded in 1983, the Bhimeshwar temple beside the dam, and the Butterfly Research Centre on the Jones Estate, where Frederick Smetacek Senior and Peter Smetacek recorded 243 butterfly species. It is the seat of the ICAR-Central Institute of Coldwater Fisheries Research, founded in 1987, and of tourism on the lakes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhimtal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Baz Bahadur Chand, a contemporary of the Mughal emperor Shah Jahan, extended the kingdom to the Tibetan border and is credited with building temples, including the Bhimeshwar temple at <b>Bhimtal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kumaon-kingdom">Kumaon Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
